# Changing a gate is tier 1

These files judge every other change in the repository, so a mistake here is invisible until it waves
a real failure through.

- **Every job always runs and decides internally whether it has work to do.** Never add a job-level
  `if`. A skipped job reports a `skipped` conclusion, and a skipped job that the ruleset requires
  leaves pull requests permanently unmergeable. Guard *inside* the job and log a notice instead.
- **A guard names the artefact the job actually consumes**, not a neighbouring directory. Guarding an
  OpenAPI drift check on `services/brain` existing, rather than on the generator script existing,
  turned a green gate red the moment an empty Python skeleton landed — for a reason unrelated to the
  change under review.
- **A gate for a component that does not exist yet logs a notice and passes**, then begins enforcing
  automatically once the component lands.
- **Never make a required check stricter in one step.** A check that has never enforced anything reds
  every open pull request the moment it starts. Add the reporter, then measure and report, then
  ratchet — as issues #178, #179 and #180 do for diff coverage.
- Pin third-party actions to a commit SHA, matching the existing style.
- A required status check only becomes enforceable after a workflow has reported it once, so apply a
  ruleset change after the first pull request has run it.
